y that need  not be The Preserve of rich countries  universal  health coverage is everyone getting all  the health services they need without  suffering financial hardship obviously  it depends on a country's level of  wealth as to what package of services  you can provide to everyone even  low-income countries have at times been  able to introduce a Universal Health  System  so our other developing countries  following in Thailand's footsteps  the good news is that Global HealthCare  coverage people accessing essential  Services has improved  the bad news is this is only half the  battle far too many people are still  having to pay unaffordable and crippling  costs  the proportion of households spending  over 10 percent of their income on  health has risen by 40 since 2000.  this isn't true Universal care which  urgently needs administering in higher  doses across the world what you want to  move away from is out of pocket spending  you know sort of people buying and  selling some Services which is a  wretched way to to fund a health system  without that Financial Protection then a  lot of poor people will never Access to  Health Services  so what needs to change for more  countries to be able to cough up for  Affordable Health Care like Thailand  the answer hinges on public financing or  how countries extract the money to pay  for it  [Music]  usually three sources provide the  necessary cash injections  there's mandatory taxes collected in the  workplace known as social insurance  contributions General taxation with a  slice of all tax receipts earmarked for  health care and other state income like  selling natural resources  countries tend to prescribe all three  but the precise dose depends on their  circumstances like the makeup of their  economy and their working population  [Music]  it sounds all well and good but what  about the poorest countries with limited  income and few workers in the formal  economy who can pay taxes  one nation has led the way here w